# Windows batch files must reach the working tree with CRLF line endings.
#
# cmd.exe mis-parses a batch file that has LF-only line endings when the file
# also contains a non-ASCII byte and the console codepage is UTF-8 (65001):
# it resumes parsing partway into the following line. Measured 2026-08-15 on
# tools/restart_one.bat, whose second line then parsed as
#     M restart_one.bat <kill_port|0> ...
# so cmd read the `<` as a redirection and killed the script on line 2 with
# "< was unexpected at this time." -- from PowerShell (codepage 65001) but not
# from Git Bash (codepage 437), which is what made it look invocation-specific.
#
# core.autocrlf=true already produced CRLF for this machine, but it is a
# per-user setting and it does NOT re-write a file an editor has replaced
# in place with LF (git normalizes both directions on comparison, so the
# damaged file still reads as clean). This rule pins the requirement in the
# repo instead. Guarded by tools/tests/test_batch_line_endings.py.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
